# Depth-Anything ONNX model for parallax (large, fetched per-node, never commit)
assets/depth_anything_v2_vits.onnx
*.onnx
nostr.json

# built by install.sh --turn / Dockerfile (not committed)
turnserver/pion-turn
# streamserver/ holds only generated artifacts (the MediaMTX binary, generated config, and MediaMTX's
# auto-generated WebRTC/MoQ certs) — never track any of it.
streamserver/
# The bundled instance's GENERATED config (install.sh --searxng): a settings.yml with this node's
# secret_key in it, plus the port marker and the branding copy. Per-node and secret — never track it.
# ANCHORED: a bare `searxng/` also matches docker/searxng/, which is the settings TEMPLATE both
# install paths read — untracked, `./install.sh --searxng` aborts on every node and the compose
# service bind-mounts a path that doesn't exist (Docker then creates a DIRECTORY there and the
# container crash-loops), so every node silently falls through to the public instance.
/searxng/
# The bundled CalDAV server's WORKING DIRECTORY: a cache of what the relay holds, and it holds
# people's actual calendars. Per-node runtime state — never track it. (The record is the encrypted
# Nostr events; this directory is rebuilt from them.)
/caldav-data/

# Instance custom emoji (Pleroma/Akkoma-style packs, Admin → Site Settings → Custom emoji
# directory). Operator content — hundreds of MB of images, per-node, never committed.
assets/emoji/
.cache/

# Desktop app build outputs. www/ is assembled from static/js/client by desktop/build-www.sh on every
# build, and resources/tor is the Tor Expert Bundle the CI workflow downloads per platform — both are
# derived, and the tor binaries are tens of MB of third-party build artifacts.
desktop/www/
desktop/resources/
# The Capacitor bundle, same story (mobile/build-www.sh assembles it per build).
mobile/www/

# Stray ffmpeg captures: probing a live path by hand (ffmpeg -i rtsp://... -t 4 -c copy out.mkv)
# drops the file in the repo root, and it is never something we want committed.
/*.mkv

# The SSH keeper's unix socket (app/services/ssh_keeper.py). A socket is not a file git can
# track, and `git add -A` should not be trying.
.run/
RELEASE_NOTES.md
